WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.613
My husband and I started this channel during

00:00:07.613 --> 00:00:15.400
lockdown as a way to bring joy and positivity

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.102
during a dark time. And from that and the years

00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:20.920
that have followed, I've realized there's nothing

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.036
I love more than a bit of painstakingly crafted

00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:27.020
silliness. And a big part of that painstaking

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.081
craft is of course editing. Now, editing is an art

00:00:32.081 --> 00:00:36.940
form. It takes a million tiny decisions to shape

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.246
how a video feels. A one hour shoot

00:00:40.246 --> 00:00:43.646
can be 10 hours or more in the edit.

00:00:43.646 --> 00:00:46.480
So what if we could speed that

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.944
process along without sacrificing any of our

00:00:48.944 --> 00:00:49.896
creative control?

00:00:49.896 --> 00:00:51.800
Which takes me to the announcement

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.552
I want to share with you today.

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:55.135
A new conversational editing

00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:57.000
assistant is coming to both short

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.845
apps and YouTube create. Yeah.

00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:02.689
Conversational editing is like

00:01:02.689 --> 00:01:05.060
having a creative partner

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.433
right in the edit with you. Rather than just

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.860
selecting a bunch of content and then handing

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.470
over the creative keys to a computer,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.798
it works things through with you.

00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:16.280
Stepping in when it's

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.484
needed and stepping back when it's not.

00:01:18.484 --> 00:01:21.140
You basically just use natural language to kind

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.258
of explain the type of edit you're going for.

00:01:24.258 --> 00:01:26.059
And it'll help you develop

00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:27.860
your first draft. But this

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.446
isn't a one and done situation. You can continue

00:01:30.446 --> 00:01:33.140
going back and forth with the agent to perfect the

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:36.569
edit, working on everything from typography to music,

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:38.704
or even nailing that perfect hook

00:01:38.704 --> 00:01:40.580
to grab attention on a short.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.642
Let me show you how it works. I select clips

00:01:44.642 --> 00:01:47.965
from my camera roll. And then I say,

00:01:47.965 --> 00:01:52.580
find and trim the best takes of me in each outfit.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:56.345
See, it's able to identify the different

00:01:56.345 --> 00:02:00.580
colors in each outfit and pull them together.
